  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/... \nDry westerly flow is expected to persist through at least \nSaturday with large scale troughing remaining over the Pac NW \nwhile the subtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east \nTX into the interior of MX. Due to the presence of the ridge to \nour east, 500 mb hghts are expected to peak around 586-588 dam \nSaturday afternoon. This will result in highs topping out between \n100-105 degrees across the lower deserts (4-6 degrees above \nnormal) under mostly sunny skies and zero chance of rain. From \nSunday into early next week, there does look to be a significant \ndegree of cooling as tropical moisture arrives and rain chances \nincrease. Highs will likely fall below 100 degrees across the \nlower deserts on Sunday and continue to cool to the upper 80s and \nlower 90s Monday through Wednesday.\n\nOur attention then turns to the tropical Pacific Sunday into early \nnext week where potential moisture from two weakening hurricanes \n(Polo and Odalys) will come into play. There is still a high degree \nof uncertainty as to a) how far north the residual moisture will be \nshed out ahead of these decaying storms, and b) where the plume(s) \nof moisture will set up. Another major question mark will be just \nhow deep and progressive a developing trough of low pressure will be \nover the Intermountain West. All of these factors will have to line \nup perfectly for our forecast area to get significant rainfall. \nBoth the GEFS and EPS are telling a different story with varying \ndegrees of moisture and timing and intensity of said trough which \nis really complicating things. The latest NHC track for Hurricane\nPolo shows the storm making landfall along the coast of the Baja \nCalifornia by Monday which if aligned with the passage of the \naforementioned trough would draw a large plume of moisture into \ninterior MX and and much of AZ. NBM PoPs have already increased to\naround 20-30% with an initial band of moisture on Sunday, but \nwith the main lift arriving on Monday, PoPs are now widespread \n30-60% across our forecast area. The remnant tropical moisture may\nlinger into Tuesday before fully exiting the region on Wednesday.\nIt is also worth pointing out that some ensemble members are \nshowing significant rainfall totals in excess of 1-2\" across \nsouthcentral AZ Monday-Tuesday but there is still significant \nspread between members. Therefore, we will likely have to wait \nuntil this weekend for things to become more clear.",
